'''''Namibiocyon''''' is an extinct genus of carnivoran mammals, belonging to the family [[Amphicyonidae]] ("bear dogs"), that lived in [[Namibia]] during the [[Early Miocene]] epoch. Before the erection of this taxon in 2022, the type and only species, ''N. ginsburgi'', had been assigned to a variety of other genera. It is notable for its adaptions toward [[Hypercarnivore|hypercarnivory]].<ref name=":22">{{Cite journal |last1=Morales|first1=Jorge|last2=Pickford|first2=Martin|date=2022 |title=The taxonomic status of "Ysengrinia" ginsburgi Morales et al. 1998 (Amphicyonidae, Carnivora) from the basal middle Miocene of Arrisdrift, Namibia. |url=https://www.mme.gov.na/files/publications/b07_CommsGSN24pp1_16Morales_Pickford2022.pdf |journal=Communications of the Geological Survey of Namibia |volume=24 |pages=1–16 |via=Google Scholar}}</ref>

== History ==
Fossils of ''Namibiocyon'' were found [[Arrisdrift]] in Namibia, which has variously been dated to 17.5 Ma<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Pickford |first=Martin |date=1995 |title=Suidae from the Middle Miocene of Arrisdrift, Namibia |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288592776 |journal=Comptes Rendus de l'Académie des Sciences}}</ref> or 16 Ma,<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Van Couvering |first1=John A. |last2=Delson |first2=Eric |date=2020-12-01 |title=African Land Mammal Ages |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=40 |issue=5 |article-number=e1803340 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2020.1803340 |bibcode=2020JVPal..40E3340V |s2cid=229372221 |issn=0272-4634}}</ref> while similar remains are known from Grillental, Fiskus, Langental and Elisabethfeld, which are slightly older localities from the Northern [[Tsau ǁKhaeb Sperrgebiet National Park|Sperrgebiet]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Salesa |first=Manuel J. |date=2008 |title=Creodonta and Carnivora from the Early Miocene of the Northern Sperrgebiet, Namibia |url=https://www.academia.edu/16968115 |journal=Memoir of the Geological Survey of Namibia}}</ref> Remains belonging to this genus were first mentioned by Hendey in 1978, who identified them as ''[[Amphicyon]]'' cf. ''steinheimensis.'' It was not until 1998, that the species ''Ysengrinia ginsburgi'' was described on basis of a left [[mandible]] (AD 133) and a right [[maxilla]] (AD 604'94), with the former designated as [[holotype]].<ref name=":02">{{cite journal |last1=Morales, J. |last2=Pickford, M. |last3=Soria, D. |last4=Fraile, S. |year=1998 |title=New carnivores from the basal Middle Miocene of Arrisdrift, Namibia |journal=Eclogae Geologicae Helvetiae |volume=91 |pages=27–40}}</ref> Furthermore, [[postcrania]]l remains belonging to this species—a left [[Calcaneus|calcaneum]] (AD 611 '98) and two left [[Metatarsal bones|metatarsals]] (AD 17'00 & AD 219'97)—as well as a mandible fragment (PQAD 611) and several isolated teeth - were described in 2003.<ref name=":12">{{cite journal |last1=Morales, J. |last2=Pickford, M. |last3=Fraile, S. |last4=Salesa, M. J. |last5=Soria, D. |year=2003 |title=Creodonta and Carnivora from Arrisdrift, early Middle Miocene of southern Namibia |journal=Memoirs of the Geological Survey of Namibia |volume=19 |pages=177–194}}</ref> The referral of this species to ''Ysengrinia'' was soon questioned,<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Heizmann |first1=E. P. J |last2=Kordikova |first2=E. G. |date=2000 |title=Zur systematischen Stellung von "Amphicyon" intermedius H. v. Meyer, 1849 (Carnivora, Amphicyonidae). |url=https://www.zobodat.at/pdf/Carolinea_58_0069-0082.pdf |journal=Carolinea}}</ref> and in a revision of African amphicyonid species, Morales et al. moved it to the genus ''[[Afrocyon]]''.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last1=Morales |first1=Jorge |last2=Pickford |first2=M. |last3=Valenciano |first3=Alberto |date=2016 |title=Systematics of African Amphicyonidae, with descriptions of new material from Napak (Uganda) and Grillental (Namibia) |journal=Journal of Iberian Geology|volume=42|issue=2|pages=131–150|hdl=10261/137042 |url=https://digital.csic.es/handle/10261/137042|issn=1698-6180}}</ref> This was challenged by Morlo et al., who instead referred it to the genus ''[[Cynelos]]''.<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |date=2019 |title=New Amphicyonids (Mammalia, Carnivora) from Moghra, Early Miocene, Egypt |url=https://sciencepress.mnhn.fr/en/periodiques/geodiversitas/41/21 |journal=Geodiversitas |language=en |volume=41 |issue=21 |pages=731–745|doi=10.5252/geodiversitas2019v41a21 |s2cid=208284245 |last1=Morlo |first1=Michael |last2=Miller |first2=Ellen R. |last3=Bastl |first3=Katharina |last4=Abdelgawad |first4=Mohamed K. |last5=Hamdan |first5=Mohammed |last6=El-Barkooky |first6=Ahmed N. |last7=Nagel |first7=Doris |doi-access=free }}</ref> Finally, Morales et al. erected the new genus ''Namibiocyon'' for this species in 2022,''<ref name=":22" />'' an assignment which was followed by Morlo et al.<ref name=":2">{{Cite journal |last1=Morlo |first1=Michael |last2=Nengo |first2=Isaiah O. |last3=Friscia |first3=Anthony |last4=Mbogo |first4=Winfred |last5=Miller |first5=Ellen R. |last6=Russo |first6=Gabrielle A. |date=2023-01-26 |title=Presence of a giant amphicyonid and other carnivores (Mammalia) from the Middle Miocene of Napudet, Kenya |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=42 |issue=2 |article-number=e2160643 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2022.2160643 |s2cid=256354136 |issn=0272-4634|doi-access=free }}</ref>

The species name ''ginsburgi'' honors the palaeontologist [[:fr:Léonard Ginsburg|Leonard Ginsburg]].<ref name=":02" />

== Description ==
Although larger than a [[wolf]],<ref name=":3">{{Cite journal |last=Guérin |first=Claude |date=2003 |title=Palaeoecological study of Arrisdrift Mammals |url=http://the-eis.com/elibrary/sites/default/files/downloads/literature/Palaeoecological%20study%20of%20Arrisdrift%20mammals.pdf |journal=Memoir Geol. Surv. Namibia}}</ref> ''Namibiocyon'' was a medium-sized amphicyonid, bigger than species such as ''[[Ysengrinia|Ysengrinia gerandiana]]'' and ''[[Cynelos|Cynelos lemanensis]]'',<ref name=":02" /> but slightly smaller than ''[[Afrocyon]]''<ref name=":0" /> and ''C. anubisi''<ref name=":1" /> (which is sometimes moved into a separate genus, ''[[Mogharacyon]]'').<ref name=":22" /> The [[dentition]] of this genus is trenchant and adapted towards hypercarnivory,<ref name=":1" /> with a reduced p4 [[Cusp (anatomy)|protocone]], a high length/breadth index,<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Morlo |first1=Michael |last2=Miller |first2=Ellen R. |last3=El-Barkooky |first3=Ahmed N. |date=2007-03-12 |title=Creodonta and Carnivora from Wadi Moghra, Egypt |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=27 |issue=1 |pages=145–159 |doi=10.1671/0272-4634(2007)27[145:CACFWM]2.0.CO;2 |s2cid=86235694 |issn=0272-4634}}</ref> narrow m2 [[Molar (tooth)#Tribosphenic|talonid]], a strongly reduced m1 [[Glossary of mammalian dental topography|metaconid]] and a m1 paracristid pointing more anteriorly than in other amphicyonids.<ref name=":2" />

Among its defining features are the small [[diastema]] occurring between the third and fourth [[premolar]]s, and the [[Infraorbital foramen|infra-orbital foramen]] is located above it. The strongly reduced P3 is formed of a single, rather low cusp. The P4 possesses a greatly reduced protocone, a short metastyle and completely lacks a parastyle. However, it has a very voluminous paracone, which has a clearly defined anterior crest, that is interrupted by an incision at mid-height. M1's metacone possesses a strong external cingulum at its base, which weakens at the base of the larger paracone. This cingulum is separated from the protocone by a deep slit. In comparison to the weaker parastyle, the metastyle is high and well-marked. The similar sized paraconule and metaconule both back onto a dune-shaped protocone. Its trigon possesses a deep central valley.<ref name=":02" /> The size of its [[Alveolus|alveoli]] in the maxilla suggests that its M2 was not reduced, clearly differentiating if from ''Ysengrinia''.<ref name=":0" />

''Namibiocyon'' possesses an extensive [[mandibular symphysis]], which has a rugose surface, and a high [[Mandible|horizontal ramus]]. Two [[Foramen|foramina]] occur in the mandible, one of which is aligned with the gap between m1 and m2, whereas the other is below p3. Its canine has been described as [[Felidae|feloid]], with two well-marked crests, which are located posterior and lingual, respectively. The poorly preserved lower premolars are separated from each other by diastema, among which the anterior ones are more developed than the posterior ones. The p4 possesses a low anterior accessory cusplet and a slightly higher posterior accessory cusplet, as well as a strong internal cingulum. The relatively short m1 has a high trigonid and talonid. The tall and narrow hypoconid dominates the former and occupies most of the latter. Both the almost vertical paraconid and the very high protoconid are narrow. The rather small metaconid is located close to the protoconid. The m2 is much smaller than m1, with a high and vertical external wall and a low lingual one. The trigonid lacks a paraconid, and is dominated by the large, high protoconid, which is located in front of the metaconid. A low antero-internal valley is located in the anterior part of the trigonid. The talonid possesses a narrow posterior margin, and consists predominantly of an enormous hypoconid.<ref name=":02" />

The proportions of the [[calcaneus]] are quite unlike those of ''[[Amphicyon]]'', with a proportionally greater proximo-distally articular zone. It is slightly concave in lateral view, and here is a marked area for the [[Quadratus plantae muscle|musculus quadratus plantae]], reaching almost to the tuber calcis, in its central zone. There is a slight fossa for ligamentary attachment near the anterior margin in front of the insertion of the muscle. As in other amphicyonids, the talar facet of the coracoid process, whereas the medio-laterally elongated sustentaculum tali is less distally displaced than in ''Amphicyon major'', and its posterior groove has more marked borders. The proximal half of its anterior border is also thinner than in that species. Its coracoid process also possesses a long, narrow and convex facet, below which a rugose surface is developed in the distal half of its anterior surface. The more or less circular tuber calcis has a slightly rugose surface, with a smooth circular area on its anterior margin, which is surrounded by the medial, lateral and posterior borders, where the calcaneal tendon inserts. Whereas the articular facet for the cuboid of A. major is elliptical and slightly concave in distal view, that of ''Namibiocyon'' is semicircular. The two preserved metatarsals are short and robust, and resemble those of ''Amphicyon''.<ref name=":12" />

== Classification ==
''Namibiocyon ginsburgi'' was originally classified as a species of ''Ysengrinia'', a taxon with similar hypercarnivorous adaptions now referred to the subfamily [[Thaumastocyoninae]].<ref name=":13">{{cite journal | url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/14772019.2021.1910868 | doi=10.1080/14772019.2021.1910868 | title=Ammitocyon kainosgen. Et sp. Nov., a chimerical amphicyonid (Mammalia, Carnivora) from the late Miocene carnivore traps of Cerro de los Batallones (Madrid, Spain) | year=2021 | last1=Morales | first1=Jorge | last2=Abella | first2=Juan | last3=Sanisidro | first3=Oscar | last4=Valenciano | first4=Alberto | journal=Journal of Systematic Palaeontology | volume=19 | issue=5 | pages=393–415 | s2cid=235363054 }}</ref> Meanwhile, ''Namibiocyon'', and all other amphicyonids known from Africa, are members of the tribe [[Amphicyoninae|Amphicyonini]].<ref name=":22" /> Its strongly pronounced hypercarnivorous features have led some researchers to suggest that it was ancestral to the genus ''[[Myacyon]]'', which was widespread throughout Africa in the middle to late Miocene.<ref name=":02" /><ref name=":2" /> Opposed to the trend seen in several of its African members, Eurasian species of this tribe showcase adaptions towards [[Mesocarnivore|mesocarnivory]] and bone-crushing,<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Solé |first1=Floréal |last2=Lesport |first2=Jean-François |last3=Heitz |first3=Antoine |last4=Mennecart |first4=Bastien |date=2022-06-15 |title=A new gigantic carnivore (Carnivora, Amphicyonidae) from the late middle Miocene of France |journal=PeerJ |language=en |volume=10 |article-number=e13457 |doi=10.7717/peerj.13457 |pmid=35726261 |pmc=9206431 |issn=2167-8359 |doi-access=free }}</ref> suggesting that this tribe's radiation was likely considerably more complex than usually acknowledged.<ref name=":22" />

== Palaeoecology ==
The locality of Arrisdrift likely represents a side channel of the Proto-[[Orange River]], which was only occasionally filled with flowing water, perhaps when the river overflowed during the rainy season. The rest of the time it was a shallow pool. The presence of ''[["Crocodylus" gariepensis|<nowiki/>'Crocodylus' gariepensis]]'' and giant tortoises indicate a more tropical climate than found in the area today.<ref name=":23">{{Cite journal |last=Anton |first=Mauricio |date=2003 |title=Reconstructing fossil mammals from Arrisdrift (17-17.5 Ma), Namibia |journal=Memoir of the Geological Survey of Namibia}}</ref> The habitat was likely a bushy, rather wooded savanna, with a [[Gallery forest|gallery-forest]] along the river.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Senut |first1=Brigitte |last2=Pickford |first2=Martin |last3=Ségalen |first3=Loïc |date=2009-08-01 |title=Neogene desertification of Africa |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1631071309000790 |journal=Comptes Rendus Geoscience |series=Histoire climatique des déserts d'Afrique et d'Arabie |language=en |volume=341 |issue=8 |pages=591–602 |doi=10.1016/j.crte.2009.03.008 |issn=1631-0713}}</ref>

In comparison to other predators of this ecosystem, ''Namibiocyon'' was of intermediate size, considerably smaller than its larger relative ''[[Amphicyon]]'' and the giant [[Hyaenodonta|hyaenodont]] [[Hyainailouros|''Hyainailourus'']], but larger than the [[Felidae|felids]] ''[[Diamantofelis]]'' and ''[[Namafelis]]'', the [[Viverridae|viverrid]] ''[[Orangictis]]'', the [[Stenoplesictidae|stenoplesicitid]] ''[[Africanictis]]'', the large [[Mustelidae|mustelid]] ''[[Namibictis]]'' and the [[Hyainailouridae|hyainailourid]] ''[[Buhakia]]''.<ref name=":3" /><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Morales |first1=Jorge |last2=Pickford |first2=Martin |date=2017 |title=New hyaenodonts (Ferae, Mammalia) from the early miocene of napak (Uganda), koru (Kenya) and grillental (Namibia) |journal=Fossil Imprint|volume=73|issue=3–4|pages=332–359|url=https://digital.csic.es/handle/10261/195968 |doi=10.13039/501100000780 |hdl=10261/195968 |issn=2533-4069}}</ref> Therefore, it likely preyed on medium-sized mammals,<ref name=":02" /> such as the [[Suidae|suids]] ''[[Namachoerus]]'' and ''[[Nguruwe]],''<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Pickford |first=M. |date=1995-11-19 |title=Suidae (Mammalia, Artiodactyla) from the early Middle Miocene of Arrisdrift, Namibia: Namachoerus (gen. nov.) moruoroti, and Nguruwe kijivium |url=http://the-eis.com/elibrary/sites/default/files/downloads/literature/Suidae%20from%20the%20Middle%20Miocene%20of%20Arrisdrift.pdf |journal=Comptes Rendus de l'Académie des Sciences, Série IIA |language=en |volume=3204 |pages=319–326}}</ref> the [[Chevrotain|tragulid]] ''[[Dorcatherium]]'',<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Morales |last2=Soria |last3=Sánchez |last4=Quiralte |last5=Pickford |date=2003 |title=Tragulidae from Arrisdrift, basal Middle Miocene, Southern Namibia |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/286612441 |journal=Memoir of the Geological Survey of Namibia}}</ref> the small [[Bovidae|bovid]] ''[[Namacerus]],'' or juveniles of the [[Climacoceratidae|climatoceratid]] ''[[Orangemeryx]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Morales |last2=Soria |last3=Nieto-Diaz |last4=Peláez-Campomanes |last5=Pickford |date=2003 |title=New data regarding Orangemeryx hendeyi Morales et al., 2000, from the type locality, Arrisdrift, Namibia |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/236011193 |journal=Memoir of the Geological Survey of Namibia}}</ref>'' Other inhabitants include the [[Elephant shrew|macroscelidid]] ''[[Myohyrax]]'', the [[Pedetes|springhare]] ''[[Megapedetes]]'', the [[Lagomorpha|lagomorph]] ''[[Australagomys]]''  the diminutive [[aardvark]] ''[[Myorycteropus]]''<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Martin |first=Pickford |date=2003-08-08 |title=Minute species of Orycteropus from the early middle Miocene at Arrisdrift, Namibia |url=http://the-eis.com/elibrary/sites/default/files/downloads/literature/Minute%20species%20of%20Orycteropus%20from%20the%20early%20Middle%20Miocene%20at%20Arrisdrift.pdf |journal=Memoir - Geological Survey of Namibia |language=en |volume=19 |pages=195–198}}</ref><ref name="L09">{{cite journal |last1=Lehmann |first1=T. |year=2009 |title=Phylogeny and systematics of the Orycteropodidae (Mammalia, Tubulidentata) |journal=Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=155 |issue=3 |pages=649–702 |doi=10.1111/j.1096-3642.2008.00460.x |doi-access=free}}</ref> and the [[proboscidea]] ''[[Afromastodon]]'' and ''[[Prodeinotherium]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Martin |first=Pickford |date=2003-03-07 |title=New Proboscidea from the Miocene strata in the lower Orange River valley, Namibia |url= |journal=Memoir - Geological Survey of Namibia |language=en |volume=19 |pages=207–256}}</ref>

